<div class="page-header">
  <h1 class="page-header__title">What Can You Deploy With HelloDeploy?</h1>
  <p class="page-header__subtitle">
    HelloDeploy is being built as a general web application deployment platform, but a runtime
    is only listed as supported once a complete deployment has been tested end to end. Do not
    assume a language or framework works because it is popular — check the table below.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>What the statuses mean</h2>
    <dl class="detail-list">
      <dt>Supported</dt>
      <dd>Verified through a complete deployment: install, build, start, routing, and logs.</dd>
      <dt>Testing</dt>
      <dd>HelloDeploy recognises the framework, but it is still being evaluated and may not be ready for general use.</dd>
      <dt>Planned</dt>
      <dd>Intended, but there is no deployment path for it today.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card mt-8">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>Runtimes and frameworks</h2>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Runtime or framework</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">How it is deployed</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Static HTML, CSS and JavaScript</th>
            <td>Supported</td>
            <td>Served as static files. No build or start command needed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Node.js</th>
            <td>Supported</td>
            <td>Runs your start command. You set the port your application listens on.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Express</th>
            <td>Supported</td>
            <td>Detected from your dependencies. Runs your start command; port 3000 is assumed unless you change it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">React</th>
            <td>Supported</td>
            <td>Built to static files and served as a static site. Create React App and Vite are both recognised.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Vue</th>
            <td>Testing</td>
            <td>Built to static files and served as a static site. Vite and Vue CLI are both recognised.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Next.js</th>
            <td>Testing</td>
            <td>Runs as a Node server. Requires <code class="code-inline">output: 'standalone'</code> in your Next.js config — see below.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Python, Flask, FastAPI, Django</th>
            <td>Planned</td>
            <td>No deployment path yet.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">PHP, Laravel</th>
            <td>Planned</td>
            <td>No deployment path yet.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Java, Spring Boot</th>
            <td>Planned</td>
            <td>No deployment path yet.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Your own Dockerfile</th>
            <td>Planned</td>
            <td>HelloDeploy builds the container image itself. A Dockerfile in your repository is ignored.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="card mt-8">
  <div class="card__body">
    <h2>Things worth knowing before you deploy</h2>

    <h3>Read the port from the environment</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      HelloDeploy sets a <code class="code-inline">PORT</code> environment variable when your
      application starts, matching the application port on your project. Read it
      (<code class="code-inline">process.env.PORT</code>) rather than hardcoding a number.
      <code class="code-inline">PORT</code> is managed by the platform, so it cannot be set as
      one of your own environment variables — nor can
      <code class="code-inline">NODE_ENV</code>, <code class="code-inline">HOST</code> or
      <code class="code-inline">HOSTNAME</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Dependencies install with npm</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      Builds run <code class="code-inline">npm ci</code>, which needs a
      <code class="code-inline">package-lock.json</code> committed to your repository. If your
      project locks with pnpm or Yarn, HelloDeploy warns you before deployment and asks for an
      npm lockfile.
    </p>

    <h3>Next.js needs standalone output</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      HelloDeploy deploys Next.js from its standalone build output, so your
      <code class="code-inline">next.config</code> needs
      <code class="code-inline">output: 'standalone'</code>. Without it, the build produces
      files HelloDeploy cannot start.
    </p>

    <h3>React and Vue deploy as static sites</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      A React or Vue single-page application is built and then served as static files, so it
      has no start command and no application port. If your project needs a running server,
      deploy that server as a Node or Express project.
    </p>

    <h3>HelloDeploy manages containerisation</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      HelloDeploy generates the container image for your project. A Dockerfile in your
      repository is ignored, and arbitrary container images, Docker Compose files, and
      privileged containers are not supported.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

